    "chapter_label": "Microorganisms: friend and foe",
    "subtopic_id": "science/grade_08/ch_02/H007",
    "stem": "Vivaan watches a Paramecium under the school microscope. It swims, takes in food, and divides on its own. A classmate says it cannot be a complete living organism because it has only one cell. Vivaan also studies one muscle cell on a chart of the human body, and recalls that some microorganisms such as bacteria are unicellular.\n\nAnswer both parts.",
    "item_type": "structured",
    "options": {},
    "statements": [],
    "parts": [
      {
        "id": "i",
        "text": "Which claim is correct?",
        "marks": 1,
        "options": [
          {
            "id": "A",
            "text": "The Paramecium is a complete living organism even though it is one cell; the muscle cell from the human body chart is not itself a complete organism."
          },
          {
            "id": "B",
            "text": "The Paramecium is an incomplete living organism because it has only one cell."
          },
          {
            "id": "C",
            "text": "The muscle cell from the human body chart is itself a complete organism."
          },
          {
            "id": "D",
            "text": "All microorganisms are unicellular."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ii",
        "text": "Give one reason for your choice.",
